  "unit": "u05-numbers-time",
  "title": "Tens to 100",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "The tens: **দশ (10), বিশ (20), ত্রিশ (30), চল্লিশ (40), পঞ্চাশ (50), ষাট (60), সত্তর (70), আশি (80), নব্বই (90), একশ (100).**\n\nEach ten echoes a small number, which helps — and also makes them easy to mix up:\n\n- **তিন** 3 · **তেরো** 13 · **ত্রিশ** 30\n- **চার** 4 · **চৌদ্দ** 14 · **চল্লিশ** 40\n- **পাঁচ** 5 · **পনেরো** 15 · **পঞ্চাশ** 50\n- **সাত** 7 · **সতেরো** 17 · **সত্তর** 70\n- **আট** 8 · **আঠারো** 18 · **আশি** 80\n- **নয়** 9 · **নব্বই** 90\n\n**সাত** (7) and **ষাট** (60) are both transliterated *shat*; the difference is the 't' (tongue curled back in ষাট). Listen to the audio!\n\nHonest warning: **every number from 21 to 99 has its own form** (21 is *ekush*, 99 is *niranobboi*). Don't try to learn them all at once — the tens get you through most prices and ages.\n\n**একশ** (100) is also spelled **একশো**. **হাজার** = 1,000. Above that, South Asia counts in *lakh* (100,000) and *crore* (10 million), so 1,00,000 is written with commas that way.",
